Agent-surface detection rules — Cisco AI Defense scanners and Agent Threat Rules
------------------------------------------------------------------------
`packages/contract/src/agent-rules.ts` holds scanent's own regular
expressions, each re-authored from a public detection rule and citing it in
its `source` field. No rule file is copied verbatim. The sources are:

  cisco-ai-defense/mcp-scanner    Apache License 2.0, Copyright (c) Cisco
  (https://github.com/cisco-ai-defense/mcp-scanner)   Systems, Inc.
  cisco-ai-defense/skill-scanner  Apache License 2.0
  cisco-ai-defense/a2a-scanner    Apache License 2.0

  Agent-Threat-Rule/agent-threat-rules   MIT License
  (https://github.com/Agent-Threat-Rule/agent-threat-rules), which the
  Cisco skill-scanner bundles and which some scanent rules (`atr:*`) are
  re-authored from.

Version 1 re-authors the MCP rules only. The detection logic is scanent's;
the sources are credited for the attack patterns they describe.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Security audit method — cloudflare/security-audit-skill (MIT License)
------------------------------------------------------------------------
`packages/scanner/vendor/cloudflare-security-audit/` holds, unmodified, the
Markdown instruction texts of Cloudflare's security-audit skill
(https://github.com/cloudflare/security-audit-skill, path
`skills/security-audit/`) at commit
c1c8a8c1471069fb0e188eeaff69b8e8db6564a8, with its licence:

  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025-2026 Cloudflare, Inc.

`SOURCE.json` beside them pins each file by SHA-256. The vulnerability
scanner follows the method they describe (reconnaissance, hunting by attack
class, adversarial validation, and the verdicts confirmed, needs_validation
and rejected), and its attack classes are the ones `ATTACK-CLASSES.md` names.
The skill's JavaScript validators are not included. scanent never runs the
code it reviews, so every step of the method that would need running code
ends as needs_validation.

The vulnerability scanner labels its findings with the reporting vocabulary
SnailSploit's Claude-Red (https://github.com/SnailSploit/Claude-Red, MIT
License) uses: CWE ids, OWASP Top 10 and API Security Top 10 categories, and
MITRE ATT&CK technique ids. No Claude-Red text is included.

